WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:12.420
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: Great
to see everybody out here

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.170
this morning.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.570
My name is RJ Pestritto.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.370
I am the Graduate
Dean at the college

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.200
and a Professor of Politics.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.920
And I am chairing this
panel on the future

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.680
of the Democratic Party.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.660
We have three very
distinguished panelists.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.930
Each of them is going to speak
for no more than 15 minutes,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.380
and I'll introduce
each speaker as he's

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.570
about to come up to the podium.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.173
And then following the
initial presentations,

00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:45.090
we'll have some discussion
among the panelists

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:50.160
and throw things open to
questions from the audience.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.040
We're going to begin
with Douglas Schoen.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.190
Douglas Schoen has been
one of the most well-known

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.420
Democratic campaign
consultants for over 40 years.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.440
He was a founding partner and
former Principal Strategist

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.120
for the polling and consulting
firm Penn, Schoen and Berland.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.030
And he's also the founder and
principal of Douglas E. Schoen,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.370
LLC, in New York City.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.430
His political clients have
included former President Bill

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.970
Clinton as well as former
New York City Mayor Michael

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.560
Bloomberg.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.380
As you probably know,
he's a regular contributor

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.695
to The Wall Street Journal,
to The Washington Post,

00:01:31.695 --> 00:01:35.800
and various other newspaper
and online publications,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.010
as well as Fox News.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.832
Please join me in
welcoming Douglas Schoen.

00:01:40.832 --> 00:01:43.242
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.180
DOUGLAS SCHOEN:
Well, first of all,

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.910
thank you for the
kind introduction.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.310
I'm very pleased to be here.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:58.330
And I think in answer
to one of the questions

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:03.760
that I frequently get, yes, I
am still a Democrat, barely.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.240
And I say that, and
one of the problems

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.980
I have doing this panel with
two extraordinary minds,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.820
particularly someone
in Bill Galston I

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.870
say that, Andrew, particularly
because he frequently writes

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.570
in The Journal what I'm
thinking before I've even

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:26.110
had a chance to
crystallize my thoughts.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:31.240
So Bill, if I plagiarize
you or otherwise take

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.900
a bit of your thunder,
I would apologize.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.430
But I grew up in an era where
the two parties were relatively

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.910
close, that there were
moderate Republicans

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.920
and conservative Democrats.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.810
I count myself now as a moderate
to conservative Democrat,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:58.005
more in the, I'd say, Scoop
Jackson, Hubert Humphrey mold.

00:02:58.005 --> 00:02:59.860
There are very few of us left.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:03.430
I wrote down in my
notes, a dinosaur.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.030
And while I hope that's
an exaggeration, since I

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.760
see none of you laughing,
I realize it's not

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.550
too much of an exaggeration.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:21.070
We live in a world now
that I find as a Democrat,

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:26.650
and I dare say as an American,
deeply distressing and deeply

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:33.910
disorienting because no matter
what your view is of policy

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.460
outcomes, we are all Americans.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.500
We have American
interests and we

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.130
have, in my judgment,
an overarching need

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:45.790
to pursue them as Americans,
not as conservatives,

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:50.530
not as moderates, not as
liberals, but as Americans.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.530
You hear that occasionally,
less so than you used to,

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.290
but the number of people
who really believe that

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.390
is precious few.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:04.480
And as a Democratic centrist who
is hawkish on foreign policy,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.850
I find myself a very,
very lonely man.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.500
I also find myself
lonely in another area.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.350
And again, Bill, I apologize.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.920
Bill's written a column
on what I'm about to say,

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.690
and so I don't mean
to be too impertinent.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:26.030
And I'll put it in the
form of a question.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.140
How many of you have
heard Democrats this year

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.850
on the campaign trail of any
ideological stripe talking

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:37.990
about job creation,
growing the economy,

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:43.270
preparing people for
technological jobs or jobs

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:43.930
that may exist?

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.460
I haven't heard it.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.830
And it startles me because when
you look at the polling data,

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.960
if the economy is
not number one,

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.240
it's certainly number 2 or 3.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.780
And if we get any
sort of downturn

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:03.130
into the 2020 election,
it is absolutely the case

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:08.940
that the economy will be front
and center, the top issue.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.230
To my way of thinking,
in the absence

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.010
of any discussion of
growth and job creation,

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:21.430
we focus to a degree that
I find almost unimaginable

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.340
on redistribution of wealth.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:31.210
The comprehensive set of
policies from increasing taxes

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:38.080
on the wealthy, wealth
taxes, increasing inheritance

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.170
taxes, to me offers a very
clear and compelling narrative

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:51.520
of what the Democratic
Party has come to stand for.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.240
And every new policy
that gets articulated,

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:58.910
if there is a huge price
tag associated with it,

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.350
such as the Green
New Deal, you hear,

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.560
well, the wealthy
should pay more.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:11.630
I daresay that that is
neither an economic strategy,

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:14.630
nor is it a strategy
on climate change,

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:17.320
nor is it something
that, in my mind,

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:22.160
is reasonable and
responsible at all.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.940
The other thing that's not
reasonable and responsible

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.350
to me is where we stand with
the federal debt and deficit.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:33.610
And as one of those in the mid
'90s who pushed Bill Clinton

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:36.940
very hard to do a balanced
budget to reduce the debt

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:41.290
and deficit, to compromise with
Newt Gingrich-- and I believe,

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.920
if I'm right, Bill, left the
country with a small surplus

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:48.850
when the Clinton
administration was over--

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:53.290
I am appalled that we
have a deficit now that

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.300
is in the trillions of dollars.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.210
And there is nobody--

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.810
and if there is somebody and
I missed it, I apologize.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.440
But I don't hear anyone of
any real significance talking

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.060
about that or the implications
of that for social programs.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.240
Indeed, we tried in the
'90s to have a dialogue

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:18.470
about Social Security,
entitlement programs, Medicare,

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.860
Medicaid, Social
Security and a way

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.270
to preserve and protect
them for the future.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.610
Paul Ryan tried
in what I thought

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:29.770
was a somewhat ham-handed way.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.030
Obviously, it
didn't work so well.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:34.150
He's now seeking
other employment.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.940
But that being said, I would
think a responsible political

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:44.080
party would want to
talk about entitlements,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.190
the budget, and our future.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:50.050
And again, my party
says nothing about that.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:55.210
Rather, the emphasis is on
redistribution and something

00:07:55.210 --> 00:08:01.510
else that I find equally
disarming and disturbing,

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:06.380
which is this sectarian
conflict between the left

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.660
and what's left of the
moderate wing of the party.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.750
And the fact that
we are engaging both

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:17.410
on the presidential level and,
indeed, in terms of primaries

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.900
with the so-called
Justice Democrats

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:24.970
and the Squad in this
fratricidal process

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:30.550
will only serve to make more
challenging for the Democrats

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:37.240
the difficulty in
ultimately prevailing

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:42.970
at the presidential or
congressional level in 2020.

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.410
The question you
would obviously ask,

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:52.000
and of course, given that I'm
here, I would have to answer

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.810
is, well, what's going to happen
in the nominating process?

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:01.210
Mr. Schoen, you presumably
have been close to it.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:06.820
I did work for Mayor
Bloomberg, I guess,

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:08.350
last year and early
this year when

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:14.240
he made a determination that he
would not seek the presidency.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.460
And I certainly
understand then why

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:23.920
he said that he did not see,
with Joe Biden running, room

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:28.450
for two moderates because
the nature of the party

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:31.630
has changed to the point
where they both the energy

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:36.100
and the enthusiasm, as we saw on
the new Wall Street Journal NBC

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:44.020
poll is with the left and the
size of the number of voters

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:49.150
who will vote for a
left-wing candidate appears,

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:52.240
from what I can see
looking at that poll

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.140
today and looking at the poll
that Bill put in his column

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:59.710
in the Journal today,
to be somewhere 40%,

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:03.110
50% of the primary electorate.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:08.410
So OK, given all that,
where do we stand?

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:14.260
That poll, that NBC Wall
Street Journal poll,

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.710
has a gap of five points
between Biden and Warren.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:26.950
That to me spells dire,
dire problems for Joe Biden,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:32.620
whose challenges on the campaign
trail are well-known, seen,

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:36.220
certainly something either
others will discuss,

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:40.220
we could discuss among
ourselves or with the audience.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:41.950
But certainly, Biden
doesn't have what

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.440
you'd call growth potential.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.020
Warren clearly does.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:51.040
I don't know how many of you
have followed what she said,

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:57.460
but it is as anti-capitalist as
you would ever want to imagine.

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.780
Now, she says
she's a capitalist,

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:04.810
but says when insurance
companies charge high premiums

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:08.800
and have the temerity to try
to minimize their payouts,

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.450
that there's something
fundamentally

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:12.500
wrong with that process.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.380
I can understand why she
might want to alter the health

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.980
system, but the idea of
using a presidential election

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:25.720
to effectively eviscerate
our traditional notions

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:30.190
of capitalism and free markets
does not sound like, again,

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:33.730
the Democratic
Party that I joined

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:38.890
as a very young man, when I was
moved to involvement by John F.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:39.940
Kennedy.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:43.100
And I cite for those of
you old enough to remember,

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.130
I think it was a tax cut.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.400
I think it was '63, and
Kennedy saying, rising

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.990
tides lift all boats,
or something like that.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:56.920
Certainly, this is a very,
very different party now.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.670
But I think, given the tightness
of the polling that Bill cited

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.090
this morning in New
Hampshire, I think

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:06.910
what we've seen in
Iowa with Warren

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:08.770
within a couple of
points of Biden,

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:13.660
I think Warren, given the
enthusiasm she's generating,

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:19.510
20,000 people in
Washington Square Park,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:22.030
I guess, two days
ago, I think she's

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.670
become the favorite
for the nomination.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:27.530
And if you've noticed what
Donald Trump has done,

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:31.990
he's been attacking Biden
with his campaign videos

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:33.280
for his gaffes.

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:38.000
He's largely left
Senator Warren alone.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.390
And I think his hope
is to run against her,

00:12:40.390 --> 00:12:43.180
to run against the far
left Democratic Party,

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:48.820
and to make Warren, the Squad,
and the left effectively

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:51.010
interchangeable.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:54.920
Do I think it's likely
to be effective?

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.260
Well, let's put it this way.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:00.400
Trump is in a position where
he is eminently beatable.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:01.910
His numbers are terrible.

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:06.790
The way he conducts himself,
whether you agree with him

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:10.360
or not, is really not
what the American people

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.520
want their president to do.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.260
He flip-flops.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:17.530
His approval rating
is now in the low 40s.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:20.170
Some polls have had
it in the high 30s.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:24.590
He should be somebody
who is easy to beat.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:26.690
But I don't believe that's
going to be the case.

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:30.610
I think Warren will be a
tough, tough general election

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:33.500
candidate for the
electorate to deal with,

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.880
particularly swing
voters in the Midwest.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:41.170
And if I had to bet, I'd
bet on a close election

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:43.870
with Donald Trump
having an advantage,

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:49.150
despite all the liabilities and
political problems that he now

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:50.020
has.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:55.360
Look, if we face a recession
of the type we saw in 2008,

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:01.390
all bets are off, and we might
be looking at a Warren-Sanders

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:02.620
administration.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:04.840
But barring-- that
was also a joke.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:09.040
I don't think she'd
run with Sanders,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:11.980
but again, the absence
of any reaction

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:15.160
tells you what you think and I
think of where the Democratic

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:15.910
Party is.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.360
So I'll stop on that
note and leave it

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.030
to my colleagues
to perhaps offer

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:25.490
a more informed and thoughtful
presentation than I have.

00:14:25.490 --> 00:14:27.213
Thank you.

00:14:27.213 --> 00:14:29.568
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:32.942 --> 00:14:34.650
RONALD J. PESTRITTO:
Thank you very much.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:37.670
Next speaker will be William
A. Galston, whom many of you

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:41.000
will recognize from
his weekly column

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.220
that he writes for The
Wall Street Journal.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:47.120
He also holds the
Ezra Zilkha Chair

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:50.720
in the Brookings Institution's
Governance Studies program.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:53.420
That's where he serves
as a Senior Fellow.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:59.150
And prior to January of 2006,
he was the Saul Stern Professor

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:02.600
and acting Dean at the
School of Public Policy

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.110
at the University of Maryland.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:08.870
And I should add that Dr.
Galston was also the Deputy

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:13.820
Assistant to President Clinton
for domestic policy from 1993

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.860
to 1995.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:18.192
Please join me in welcoming him.

00:15:18.192 --> 00:15:20.572
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:26.732 --> 00:15:28.940
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Well,
it's a pleasure to be here.

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:31.960
Thank you for coming
out early in the morning

00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:34.360
to listen to these
reflections on the future

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.580
of the Democratic Party.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:38.920
Just a few comments on
what we've just heard.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:41.650
As a partially
defrocked academic,

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:44.980
I have always viewed plagiarism
as the sincerest form

00:15:44.980 --> 00:15:47.210
of flattery.

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:48.760
I am duly flattered.

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:59.350
As for budget deficits,
President Clinton

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:02.650
delivered four
years of surpluses

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:07.240
in a row, four consecutive
surpluses, to the extent

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:10.330
that the former Fed
chair, Alan Greenspan,

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.700
was wringing his hands
about the possibility

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:15.730
that the debt would be
paid down and eliminated,

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:17.770
making it very
much more difficult

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:22.630
for the global financial
system to make use of US debt

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.370
as a safe harbor.

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:29.710
That was $22
trillion dollars ago.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:33.670
I should also say that I was
at a small event with Senator

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:36.850
Michael Bennet last night, who
is also running for president,

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.470
and you would have
heard everything

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.890
you wanted to hear
about economic growth

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.630
and educational opportunity.

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:45.630
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: He's
[? at ?] 1%, if I remember?

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:47.440
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
I understand.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.880
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: So does he.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:50.890
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Yep.

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:54.400
He also understands
that Senator Gary Hart

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.820
was at 1% at a
comparable point in 1983.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:06.040
So it's-- but this being
a Hillsdale audience,

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:10.599
I will recur to
first principles.

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:15.849
As you all know,
the US Constitution

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:21.099
makes no explicit provision
for political parties.

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.640
You also know that
the founders did not

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.640
anticipate the development
of a party system,

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:31.250
and they certainly
did not want one.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:35.810
But within just a few years
after the inauguration

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:40.010
of the Constitution and the
beginning of our Constitutional

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.600
order, we had, in effect,
a two-party system.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:48.440
And with some bumps in the
road and occasional detours,

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:52.580
we've had a two-party
system ever since.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:58.070
The two-party system appears
to be our Constitutional fate.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:17:59.840
Is this bad news?

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:06.770
I think not because it turns
out that in an increasingly

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:11.300
large and diverse country,
political parties turn out

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:15.650
to be essential
ways of organizing

00:18:15.650 --> 00:18:19.580
arguments about the
future of the country.

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:22.340
And that is the
ultimate justification

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:24.950
of our party system.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.920
Or more precisely,
to propose responses

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:32.270
to the major problems facing
our country within the framework

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:37.900
of defensible interpretations
of our Constitutional order.

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:41.500
Now, this role for
the parties involves

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:48.580
in part debatable specifications
of what these problems are.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:51.010
Sometimes the
parties agree on what

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.920
the problems are,
and only disagree

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:57.130
about responses or solutions.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:18:59.410
Today, as sometimes
happens, there

00:18:59.410 --> 00:19:01.360
is disagreement
between the parties

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:04.450
both about the most
important problems

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:08.770
facing the country and the
nature of the solutions

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.990
to be offered.

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:17.470
Nonetheless, I have come neither
to bury the Democratic Party

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:21.610
nor to praise it, but to analyze
the current situation facing

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:23.920
the party and the country.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:30.070
So let me offer a list of what
I think most people would agree

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:36.070
are problems
confronting the country.

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:40.690
First, the economic
and social consequences

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:44.020
of the profound
economic transformation

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:46.420
that we have been
undergoing for decades

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:50.170
and continue to undergo
with a constant wave

00:19:50.170 --> 00:19:54.640
of technological innovation.

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:58.840
Second, the implications
of increasing

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:04.790
demographic diversity, which
is baked in to our population,

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:06.970
even if we slammed
the doors entirely

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.760
shut on immigration tomorrow.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:13.960
What are the implications of
this demographic diversity

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:19.480
for public policy and
also for social cohesion?

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:23.230
The clash, which I think is
quite a systematic clash,

00:20:23.230 --> 00:20:26.860
between what I'll
call traditional

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:30.640
and progressive
cultural outlooks.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:34.300
Shifting gears, the economic
and military implications

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:40.750
of China's rise for
America's role in the world.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.930
Authoritarian challenges
to the post-war hegemony

00:20:43.930 --> 00:20:46.030
of liberal democracy.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:49.270
And finally, and I would
add on a Constitutional

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:55.240
note, the implications of
the hollowing out of Congress

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:59.590
and the shift of power both
to the executive branch

00:20:59.590 --> 00:21:03.290
and to the judiciary.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:06.860
Now, because our large
and diverse country

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:09.710
has only two major parties,
thanks to our first

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:14.660
past the post system,
each party of necessity

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:19.580
is a coalition with internal
differences and also fissures.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:23.030
Within today's
Democratic Party, there

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:29.990
are disagreements about the
best responses to the challenges

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:32.900
that I've enumerated.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:40.040
And because of that, it is the
party's caucuses and primaries

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:44.240
that will determine the
agenda that the Democratic

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:48.680
Party presents to the
country as an alternative

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:52.910
to a Republican Party
which is now dominated,

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:58.460
as rarely before, by the
figure and the orientation

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:02.570
of the Republican president.

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:07.430
So simply put, the present
of the Democratic Party

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:12.440
is an argument about the
future of the Democratic Party.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:18.550
And what's going on
in this primary season

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:22.850
may be disorderly,
but it is not trivial

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:26.390
because it is an argument
about the future of one

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.720
of the two great and
enduring political parties

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:31.220
in this country.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:35.770
Now, what is the argument about
within the Republican Party--

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:37.940
rather the Democratic Party?

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:43.610
Well, in part it's an argument
about economics, how much

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.160
and what kind of
government intervention

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:50.390
is needed to rectify the
imbalances that have developed

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:52.560
in different sectors
of the economy,

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:56.090
and I would argue
among different regions

00:22:56.090 --> 00:22:57.110
of the country.

00:22:57.110 --> 00:23:02.780
And these regional divisions
are real and consequential and

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.450
worrying.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:08.230
So what is the right balance
of market-based mechanisms

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:11.350
and command and
control measures?

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:13.810
To what extent is the
increasing concentration

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.990
of ownership among fewer
and fewer businesses,

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:20.800
in many sectors of the
economy, a problem for growth

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:24.820
and innovation, a problem
that the antitrust division

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.490
of the Justice Department
in the Trump administration

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:30.370
is now wrestling with?

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:33.280
Can a partnership
with corporate America

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:36.730
be consistent with the
objective of economic growth

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:40.330
that benefits all
classes and regions?

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:44.470
Or should the Democratic
Party treat corporate America

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:47.485
as a foe that must
be brought to heel?

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:54.610
There are many other
divisions within the party

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:59.620
on immigration, on health care.

00:23:59.620 --> 00:24:01.420
Is it Obamacare?

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:03.520
Obamacare plus a public option?

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:06.850
Medicare for all?

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:08.560
What should we do
with our troops

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:13.060
in Afghanistan, the
so-called Endless War?

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:16.180
Should they be pulled
out unconditionally?

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:18.850
Should we demand
tougher conditions

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:23.200
than Khalilzad has so far?

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:27.160
Should we leave them
there indefinitely?

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:30.430
What about the
Trans-Pacific Partnership?

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:33.430
I think it's fair to say that
Senator Elizabeth Warren would

00:24:33.430 --> 00:24:37.690
like to get to no on the TPP
and former Vice President Biden

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:40.330
would like to get to yes.

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.970
What about the size
of the defense budget?

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:44.950
Some Democratic
candidates believe

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:48.310
that the defense budget
is about right sized,

00:24:48.310 --> 00:24:52.430
and others think
it's much too big.

00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:54.610
How much of an enemy is China?

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:57.490
Are we facing a new
Cold War or does

00:24:57.490 --> 00:25:03.530
China present us with a mix of
competition and cooperation?

00:25:03.530 --> 00:25:06.400
There is also
political disagreement.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:10.330
Does President Trump represent
an historical detour,

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:15.520
as Joe Biden would have it, or
an historical discontinuity,

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:18.520
as many other Democrats believe?

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:23.630
And in parallel with
that, is the task

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:28.890
of the Democratic Party to get
the country back to normal,

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:31.460
which is more or less the
Biden position, whatever

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:32.870
you think normal is?

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:37.310
Or is it radical
structural change,

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:40.130
which is the basic
position of the left wing

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:43.190
of the Democratic Party.

00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:44.360
What's going to happen?

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:48.770
I don't know.

00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:51.820
I genuinely don't know.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:54.820
If things continue
as they're now going,

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:58.540
we are looking, in effect,
structurally speaking,

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:02.920
at a rerun of the 2016 primary
between Hillary Clinton

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:06.490
and Bernie Sanders, although
the figures representing

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:12.410
those two different forces
in the party are different.

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:16.280
I agree that at this
point the force appears

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:19.850
to be with Senator Warren.

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:28.010
On the other hand, her appeal
is to a relatively small slice

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:32.000
of the overall demography
of the Democratic Party.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:37.910
And the African-American vote
is key after the early primaries

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:41.405
and caucuses, and there, Joe
Biden has fully 50% support,

00:26:41.405 --> 00:26:43.640
and he doesn't seem
to be losing it.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:46.780
So I think it's a fair fight.

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:49.800
There is no way of predicting
the outcome for sure.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:53.630
But I can say this.

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:57.260
History is a series of
sequential consequential

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:02.240
contingencies, each one shaping
the range of possibilities

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.250
for the next.

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:08.690
It is inevitable that the debate
in today's Democratic Party

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:14.330
will be shaped in part by the
powerful figure of Donald Trump

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:18.540
and what he represents.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.440
And the Democratic
Party is wrestling

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.110
with the question
of the best argument

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:29.720
to make against what
President Trump represents,

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:32.420
given the fact that some
of what he represents

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:35.060
overlaps with at least
some of what the Democratic

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:36.020
Party believes.

00:27:36.020 --> 00:27:38.000
Thank you very much.

00:27:38.000 --> 00:27:40.475
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:45.808 --> 00:27:47.350
RONALD J. PESTRITTO:
Well, thank you.

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:49.750
Our third speaker
this morning will

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:54.640
be Andrew Sullivan, who is a
contributing editor at New York

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:59.840
Magazine, and is also a
blogger and an author.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:03.940
He was The New Republic's
youngest Editor In Chief,

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:08.170
and he also worked for Time,
for The New York Times Magazine

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:12.200
and served as a Senior
Editor for The Atlantic.

00:28:12.200 --> 00:28:15.400
He joined New York Magazine
as a contributing editor

00:28:15.400 --> 00:28:19.330
in 2016, where he covers
politics and culture

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:22.510
and occasionally
writes features.

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:25.300
He also has two new books.

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:27.400
One is titled Keeping
the Faith, which

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:29.740
is a spiritual memoir
about the future

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:32.980
and meaning of Christianity
in the 21st century.

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:34.900
And he has a 30-year
retrospective

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:38.920
of essays and reviews
titled Thinking Out Loud.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:42.072
Please join me in
welcoming Andrew Sullivan.

00:28:42.072 --> 00:28:44.056
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:48.568 --> 00:28:50.110
ANDREW SULLIVAN:
Thank you very much.

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:53.360
Just to clear up a small,
small point of fact,

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.837
the last two books
that you cited

00:28:55.837 --> 00:28:56.920
have not yet been written.

00:28:59.710 --> 00:29:03.850
They've been put on hold
until the Trump period is

00:29:03.850 --> 00:29:05.380
concluded in some way or other.

00:29:07.923 --> 00:29:10.090
For the record, I'm not a
Democrat, never have been,

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:12.070
but neither have I
ever been a Republican.

00:29:12.070 --> 00:29:16.180
The only thing I've ever
been really is a Tory.

00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:18.340
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: Wet or dry?

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:21.430
ANDREW SULLIVAN: I was
very sort of on the rocks.

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:22.420
Let's put it that way.

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.820
So just a few thoughts
about what has been said

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:30.820
and what we're talking about.

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:35.470
I think this is an incredibly
fertile moment for the left,

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:39.260
I think, for a couple
of obvious reasons.

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:42.730
And you see this not just
here, but in Europe as well.

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:47.410
And the first is that clearly
market capitalism is failing

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:50.050
most people in the West.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:54.610
The ability to actually
raise most people's standard

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:59.680
of living to afford a house,
to afford college, all the core

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:06.094
things that people want to
make themselves a better life--

00:30:06.094 --> 00:30:10.960
I have someone whispering
in my ear here.

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:11.460
OK.

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:15.777
Where was I?

00:30:15.777 --> 00:30:17.360
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
Market capitalism.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:20.210
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Market
capitalism is failing, clearly

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:24.260
failing, and this is creating
a huge demand, especially

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:27.920
among the younger generations,
for radical reform.

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:31.940
If you look at the next
two generations' views

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:35.150
on the old issues
of right and left,

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:40.820
they are further left than
in any time since the '30s.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:44.060
They don't like capitalism
for the first time, really.

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:46.640
A generation of
Americans do not like it.

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:48.030
And you can see--

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.090
and same also in the
United Kingdom, which

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:51.380
I know a little bit about.

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:56.080
You can see it's also
the case in the UK.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:57.070
How to fix this?

00:30:57.070 --> 00:30:58.570
How to fix the fact
that everybody's

00:30:58.570 --> 00:31:01.420
working harder than ever
and only a tiny proportion

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:04.960
of people are
benefiting massively?

00:31:04.960 --> 00:31:08.920
That's the question
that people are asking.

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:10.710
And secondly, I think
the other issue that

00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:15.720
is propelling the left
is the climate crisis,

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:20.640
that as the world hurtles
towards unprecedented climate

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:27.300
turbulence and mass migration
as a consequence, obviously,

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:31.170
government has a role to
play in preventing such

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:34.070
a catastrophe from unfolding.

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:36.760
And most people, especially
those, again, under 40,

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:41.130
who will have to live in
that world, want change--

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:46.580
big change-- in order to
prevent that from happening.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:51.820
So it is, in my view,
a really fertile time.

00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:55.690
A program for
redistribution of wealth

00:31:55.690 --> 00:31:58.210
has enormous potential
as a vote winner,

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.030
whether you agree
with it or not.

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:03.130
Radical efforts to
prevent climate change

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:06.760
are increasingly popular.

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:11.250
And it should be, I
think, a walkover.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:12.540
Why [? isn't ?] it a walkover?

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.790
Why, in fact, the
Democrats and the left

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:20.130
in general are struggling
is very interesting.

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:22.440
In the last general election
in the United Kingdom,

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.700
the Labour Party led
by Jeremy Corbyn,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:30.420
the most far left individual
in the history of British

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:34.710
politics, let alone
as Prime Minister,

00:32:34.710 --> 00:32:37.530
once he laid out his
manifesto at the beginning

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:39.720
of the campaign, which
is basically massive

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:45.300
redistribution,
building of houses,

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:49.000
and an overhauling of market
capitalism in the United

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:53.760
Kingdom, he gained 20
points in the campaign.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:56.760
That is why the Tories are in
such problem because the Labour

00:32:56.760 --> 00:33:00.270
Party's message of
redistribution is actually

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:03.082
increasingly popular.

00:33:03.082 --> 00:33:04.790
I think there are two
reasons this is not

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:10.260
working for the left, and
the first is immigration,

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:12.840
that people are
prepared, I think,

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:17.210
to move to the left quite
radically on economics.

00:33:17.210 --> 00:33:22.370
But unless they're reassured
in this very turbulent time

00:33:22.370 --> 00:33:25.970
that their countries
aren't simply open borders

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:28.640
to anybody who
wants to come there,

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:31.070
they're not going
to get a hearing.

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.980
The Democrats'
position on immigration

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:39.740
is now so far to the left,
so far towards open borders,

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:44.330
that it disqualifies them in the
minds of many other people who

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:47.690
still believe that there is such
a thing as the nation state,

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:50.470
there are such
things as borders.

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:52.570
And the Democratic
Party seems to be

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:55.270
completely oblivious of this.

00:33:55.270 --> 00:33:59.640
The second reason is
intersectionality.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:05.200
It is the shift within the left,
away from the economic Marxism

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:09.820
to a cultural Marxism, in
which the various groups--

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:12.400
I don't need to explain
this at Hillsdale--

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:15.070
individuals are characterized
by their membership

00:34:15.070 --> 00:34:16.770
in certain oppressed groups.

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:20.530
Society is understood
entirely as a matter

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:23.290
of power, not freedom,
and the only thing

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:26.139
to do is to resist
those in power

00:34:26.139 --> 00:34:29.739
on behalf of those
out of it, and seeing

00:34:29.739 --> 00:34:34.389
that as the core
reality of humanity

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:39.380
divided by race, gender, sexual
orientation, gender identity.

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:43.179
And these themes are
being used and spread

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:44.659
throughout the
educational system,

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:47.780
including the elementary
school system now,

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:51.920
as a way to transform society
in the most radical lines

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:54.469
to abolish nature, to abolish
the difference between men

00:34:54.469 --> 00:34:59.140
and women, to insist that
all differences in outcome

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:02.200
in almost any line of
work or area of expertise

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:08.400
is entirely due to forms of
oppression and nothing else.

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:11.250
This ideology of course,
is now the orthodoxy

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:13.050
being taught across
American education.

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:16.680
It is the orthodoxy being
taught every day deliberately

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:18.950
by The New York Times.

00:35:18.950 --> 00:35:23.690
It is not something that most
regular, normal Americans

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:25.310
believe in.

00:35:25.310 --> 00:35:29.990
They still believe in the ideas
of opportunity, meritocracy.

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:32.330
They just want to be
able to succeed, and know

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:36.350
that in this particular economy
the chances of their succeeding

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:37.860
is extremely small.

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:43.250
In the process of the elites
of the Democratic Party left,

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:47.000
moving drastically to the
left, and indeed moving

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:51.070
towards open borders
more explicitly,

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:54.540
is a function also
of Donald Trump,

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:59.170
that Trump's genius, if
you want to call it that,

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:03.000
is to so polarize the
country to advocate

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:07.080
such extraordinary
measures on the far right,

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:11.240
that the far left has
been emboldened by this.

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:15.350
And the liberals in
the Democratic Party

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:18.560
have to say, yes, we know
we're up against this figure.

00:36:18.560 --> 00:36:21.647
Yes, we know he's doing all
these things that we despise.

00:36:21.647 --> 00:36:23.480
But let's be sensible
and let's be moderate.

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:24.500
Let's not take the bait.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:27.780
Let's not go crazy.

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:30.110
Let's not just oppose him
in every single respect

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:32.270
to the extreme.

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:35.360
That's a very, very difficult
case to make in the mood right

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:38.770
now on the left and the right.

00:36:38.770 --> 00:36:40.230
So my view, for
what it's worth--

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:42.840
and all this has
made incredibly worse

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:46.680
and has been created in
part by social media, which

00:36:46.680 --> 00:36:49.090
means the elites are really
talking to themselves.

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:51.880
They have no understanding
what's happening outside.

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:55.300
That's why they could have
nominated Hillary Clinton--

00:36:55.300 --> 00:37:00.090
an insane act given her
popularity and her credibility.

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:03.780
And now they've decided
they want Hillary 2.0,

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:05.520
but not quite as charming.

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:13.890
But they cannot help themselves.

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.080
And the liberal tradition,
liberalism itself,

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:18.000
has collapsed.

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:19.770
You see this also across Europe.

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:23.853
Center left parties have
completely imploded.

00:37:23.853 --> 00:37:26.520
And the Labour Party in Britain,
the biggest and most successful

00:37:26.520 --> 00:37:30.295
one recently, has only survived
because the far left has taken

00:37:30.295 --> 00:37:31.170
the whole thing over.

00:37:33.760 --> 00:37:35.710
So I think, short
term, we're going

00:37:35.710 --> 00:37:37.570
to have major polarization.

00:37:37.570 --> 00:37:39.880
And the Democratic Party,
if they nominate Warren,

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:40.720
will lose.

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:41.920
That's what I suspect.

00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:45.560
Of course, anything can happen.

00:37:45.560 --> 00:37:48.100
But I also think
that if we continue

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:51.760
with what we have
in power and we

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:54.550
have no reforms in
the economy, then that

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:57.310
will be a very short-term
victory for the right.

00:37:57.310 --> 00:38:01.990
And what's coming is a
landslide for the very far left.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:02.920
It'll come.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:06.040
When it comes is simply
a matter of time.

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:09.250
And whether the Democratic
Party can marshal that energy

00:38:09.250 --> 00:38:14.410
to win elections is
the real question.

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:17.360
That they're the most useless
political party in the West,

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:21.680
and they can screw it up.

00:38:21.680 --> 00:38:23.510
But the tide is with them.

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:24.836
Thank you.

00:38:24.836 --> 00:38:27.326
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:34.710
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: OK.

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:37.360
Thanks to all our panelists
for those presentations.

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:39.180
I want to give them
some opportunity

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:42.210
to respond to one another.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:45.905
Would anyone like to begin that?

00:38:45.905 --> 00:38:47.030
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: I'll begin.

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:47.530
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: Please.

00:38:47.530 --> 00:38:48.048
And if you--

00:38:48.048 --> 00:38:50.090
I think the microphones
at your seat are working,

00:38:50.090 --> 00:38:53.180
and I'll kind of referee
it from up here if I can.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:55.080
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: Bill,
how would you respond

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:57.240
to Andrew's presentation?

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:00.180
Is there an emerging
Democratic majority

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:02.960
that's just 10 or 15
years away or sooner?

00:39:08.432 --> 00:39:09.640
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Is this--

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:10.040
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: It is.

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:10.700
It is on, yes.

00:39:14.023 --> 00:39:15.440
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
I guess Andrew

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:20.570
has a much clearer
crystal ball than I do.

00:39:20.570 --> 00:39:23.960
If I wanted to be
contentious, I would

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:28.310
say that the Tories in the
UK are giving the Democrats

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:33.690
a pretty good run for their
money for uselessness,

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:36.910
but let's not go there.

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:37.910
ANDREW SULLIVAN: We can.

00:39:37.910 --> 00:39:39.350
The election's coming up.

00:39:41.733 --> 00:39:42.900
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Oh, yes.

00:39:52.520 --> 00:40:00.560
I don't share the view that
the Democratic Party has

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:02.590
capitulated to far left forces.

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:11.130
The nomination of
Hillary Clinton

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:14.070
was labeled an insane act.

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:17.930
Would it have been saner to
nominate Bernie Sanders, who is

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:20.330
the only available alternative?

00:40:20.330 --> 00:40:22.550
I don't think so.

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:31.700
And as I read, for example, the
Democratic primary electorate

00:40:31.700 --> 00:40:34.010
in New Hampshire--

00:40:34.010 --> 00:40:36.080
just in today's
Wall Street Journal

00:40:36.080 --> 00:40:41.210
I published a piece about
the views of that electorate.

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:45.580
They are not in
favor of revolution.

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:50.300
There they are in
favor of reform.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:52.310
They would like
that reform to draw

00:40:52.310 --> 00:40:58.350
on the best ideas and energy
from both political parties.

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:05.000
And I think that the view of
the Democratic Party that you

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:10.100
see if you look at it through
the prism of campus leftism

00:41:10.100 --> 00:41:18.050
is really a crazy mirror
caricature of the actual rank

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:20.390
and file of the party.

00:41:23.680 --> 00:41:27.550
No doubt the Democratic
Party of today

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:31.780
is nothing like the
Democratic Party of 1960,

00:41:31.780 --> 00:41:34.450
but I hasten to add that the
Republican Party of today

00:41:34.450 --> 00:41:38.500
is nothing like the Republican
Party of 1960 either.

00:41:38.500 --> 00:41:40.810
Both have changed.

00:41:40.810 --> 00:41:43.090
Neither has changed
in a direction

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:45.700
that I particularly like.

00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:52.920
But I think perhaps it's
my role on this panel

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:55.230
to be a defender of
the Democratic Party

00:41:55.230 --> 00:41:56.610
because no one
else in this panel

00:41:56.610 --> 00:41:59.940
seems to want to
occupy that role.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:01.980
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: But
Bill, you were defending

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:05.580
a Democratic Party in New
Hampshire that included,

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:10.650
I think, if I have it right,
30% to 40% independents.

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:13.220
Of that group, as I
remember your article,

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:15.462
30% voted for Donald Trump.

00:42:15.462 --> 00:42:16.920
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
That's correct.

00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:18.920
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: So the
group you were describing

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:22.050
is not really the Democratic
electorate as I know it

00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:24.897
or as Andrew tried to
describe it, right?

00:42:24.897 --> 00:42:25.980
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Wrong.

00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:27.438
DOUGLAS SCHOEN:
No, it isn't wrong.

00:42:27.438 --> 00:42:28.625
That's clearly right.

00:42:28.625 --> 00:42:30.750
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Let me
suggest a compromise, which

00:42:30.750 --> 00:42:34.650
is, in fact, Bill, most
regular Democratic voters

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:37.290
are not far left at all.

00:42:37.290 --> 00:42:39.420
It's irrelevant.

00:42:39.420 --> 00:42:43.230
The activist base is
selecting far left candidates

00:42:43.230 --> 00:42:46.440
to pick from, with the exception
of Biden, who is clearly

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:50.340
too old to do what he's doing.

00:42:50.340 --> 00:42:55.080
And it's those elites that
are determining the policy.

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:59.235
And I see absolutely
no one, for example,

00:42:59.235 --> 00:43:01.110
among any of the Democratic
candidates that's

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:03.660
pushed back the slightest
bit on the question,

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:07.870
say, of immigration or of
the social justice agenda.

00:43:07.870 --> 00:43:10.110
In fact, they've all
completely capitulated to it.

00:43:10.110 --> 00:43:13.140
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: I don't
agree with that, either.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:16.830
For example, Biden was
not the only Democrat

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:20.670
who pushed back
against the proposition

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:23.070
that unauthorized
border crossing should

00:43:23.070 --> 00:43:27.300
be decriminalized, not
the only one by far.

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:30.430
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Well, who else?

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:31.688
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Who else?

00:43:31.688 --> 00:43:34.230
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Because I think
they all put up their hands.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:35.770
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Bennet, no.

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:38.770
I believe Senator
Klobuchar from Minnesota

00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:41.260
did not accept that proposition,
and a couple of others.

00:43:41.260 --> 00:43:43.510
It divided the party.

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:47.170
And many of the people who
put up their hands, I think,

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:50.290
will have cause to regret
it, if they're not already

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:51.020
regretting it.

00:43:51.020 --> 00:43:53.530
I would say one other
thing, that if you

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:56.290
have lots and lots
of people fighting

00:43:56.290 --> 00:43:58.780
for half of the
party and only one

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.250
or two people fighting for
the other half of the party,

00:44:01.250 --> 00:44:03.970
there is a certain tactical
advantage to the less densely

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:06.790
populated field.

00:44:06.790 --> 00:44:12.670
And Senator Sanders is not
fading nearly fast enough

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:14.680
for the Warren forces.

00:44:14.680 --> 00:44:16.480
And as long as he
stays in the race

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:18.910
and gets above
the 15% threshold,

00:44:18.910 --> 00:44:22.210
she is going to have a very
hard time commanding a majority

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:23.500
within the Democratic Party.

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:27.880
So I guess I just have I have
a very different view of what's

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:29.690
going on.

00:44:29.690 --> 00:44:32.200
And I do not agree
with the proposition

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:35.410
that the elites are driving
the entirety of the debate

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:36.730
within the Democratic Party.

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:42.570
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: Maybe I'll
put one question to the panel

00:44:42.570 --> 00:44:45.960
before we open it up.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:50.460
I haven't heard the issue
of gun control raised

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.650
yet this morning,
but it's certainly

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:57.300
very much on the current
public policy agenda.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:59.580
And I wonder,
especially as important

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:02.430
as that is to some of
the electoral battles

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:05.070
that you're all
speaking to, if you

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:08.970
might say something about the
party in the gun control issue.

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:12.120
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
Well, I'll be happy to.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:13.710
First of all, the
Second Amendment

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:15.600
is in the Constitution.

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:18.450
And as interpreted
by the Supreme Court

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:22.360
in the Heller decision,
it's the law of the land.

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:25.650
And so the question
is, what is permissible

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:28.350
within the Constitution as
interpreted by the Supreme

00:45:28.350 --> 00:45:29.130
Court?

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:31.920
And of those things
that are permissible,

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:35.340
what is good public policy?

00:45:35.340 --> 00:45:41.340
And do Democratic proposals
map onto those contours

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:43.330
comfortably?

00:45:43.330 --> 00:45:44.940
I would say this.

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:48.120
Every single survey
that I've seen

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:52.260
shows between 85% and
90% public support

00:45:52.260 --> 00:45:56.370
for universal background
checks, every single one.

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:59.820
A majority of Democrats,
Republicans, Independents,

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:05.350
and NRA members are
all in favor of that.

00:46:05.350 --> 00:46:13.620
And I believe that the
support for that proposition

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:16.770
is strong enough so
that a candidate who

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:19.215
takes the other side
of that position

00:46:19.215 --> 00:46:20.340
is going to have a problem.

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:28.450
Do I think that the
involuntary confiscation

00:46:28.450 --> 00:46:33.160
of classes of weapons is either
constitutional or politically

00:46:33.160 --> 00:46:34.060
prudent?

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:35.590
No, I don't.

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:38.410
But there is a lot
of daylight between

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:42.160
the involuntary confiscation of
classes of weapons on the one

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:45.190
hand and the status
quo on the other,

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:47.230
and it's the task
of reasonable people

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:51.927
in both political parties
to find that sweet spot.

00:46:51.927 --> 00:46:53.510
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: Let
me try to put that

00:46:53.510 --> 00:46:56.850
in the context of campaigning.

00:46:56.850 --> 00:47:00.650
And I'm going to try to
talk in plain English.

00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:03.710
You will win a
Democratic primary

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:07.460
taking as strong a
position on gun control

00:47:07.460 --> 00:47:10.130
as you feel prudent.

00:47:10.130 --> 00:47:12.980
There is a sense,
and I think rightly,

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:17.630
of the moral urgency
of background

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:23.990
checks of a universal nature,
red flag laws, lie and try.

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:29.450
And yes, there is
65%, 70% support

00:47:29.450 --> 00:47:35.558
in the Democratic primary
for an assault weapon ban.

00:47:35.558 --> 00:47:37.850
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: So you're
predicting the nomination

00:47:37.850 --> 00:47:38.900
of Beto O'Rourke.

00:47:38.900 --> 00:47:39.903
Is that correct?

00:47:39.903 --> 00:47:42.320
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: I think I made
very clear that that is not

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:43.620
what I'm predicting.

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:45.290
And I do think you heard me.

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:53.600
Anyway, but what I would say to
Bill's point is the Republicans

00:47:53.600 --> 00:47:56.850
and their leader, Mitch
McConnell, has basically said,

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:59.210
I'm not doing anything
unless the president's

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:00.440
going to sign it.

00:48:00.440 --> 00:48:04.010
And as we sit here today,
it doesn't look to me

00:48:04.010 --> 00:48:05.610
like anything's
going to get done.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:08.390
We might-- and Bill
could be right on this--

00:48:08.390 --> 00:48:11.240
the overwhelming power of
the background check issue

00:48:11.240 --> 00:48:16.280
could be one that gets through
the Senate and the House.

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:19.220
But despite the numbers
that Bill cites,

00:48:19.220 --> 00:48:23.540
which are absolutely
correct, Mitch McConnell

00:48:23.540 --> 00:48:27.770
has been unfailingly resistant.

00:48:27.770 --> 00:48:33.080
And the real change is
in the 2018 midterms.

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:36.740
I was working for Mike
Bloomberg's PAC, I USA.

00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:38.750
We used the gun issue.

00:48:38.750 --> 00:48:43.160
We used it unabashedly
and without any apology,

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:46.070
but of course, supporting
Second Amendment,

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:50.810
and we had breakthroughs
in 21 of the 25 districts

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:52.160
we campaigned in.

00:48:52.160 --> 00:48:54.830
And in none of
those districts was

00:48:54.830 --> 00:48:59.210
support for gun control anything
but a political positive.

00:48:59.210 --> 00:49:04.790
So I think the Republicans are
behind the curve on the issue,

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:08.330
but I do also think that
the strength of a very

00:49:08.330 --> 00:49:14.210
weakened, and I dare say
allegedly corrupt NRA,

00:49:14.210 --> 00:49:20.000
is such that the other side of
the issue has been weakened.

00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:23.120
What I will say as one of
probably the few people here

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:26.150
who is debated the
issue extensively

00:49:26.150 --> 00:49:30.290
against the right is
the argument against gun

00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:33.770
control of the type
that Bill is describing,

00:49:33.770 --> 00:49:38.720
or I am both advocating
and describing is,

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:41.210
you're taking our guns away.

00:49:41.210 --> 00:49:46.460
And as specious as I think that
claim is if you're just talking

00:49:46.460 --> 00:49:49.640
about background
checks, until recently,

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:53.480
that claim and the fervor
that the right attaches to it

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:56.390
has been surprisingly effective.

00:49:56.390 --> 00:50:00.440
As I said, 2018, much
less effective, and it

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:06.110
is, in my mind, an open question
how the issue will play out

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:07.370
in 2020.

00:50:07.370 --> 00:50:12.380
But if the Republicans don't
embrace some sort of compromise

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:15.810
on getting rid of the
loopholes on background checks,

00:50:15.810 --> 00:50:19.120
I think it'll be a huge
potential Democratic issue

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:20.120
in the general election.

00:50:24.770 --> 00:50:25.770
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: OK.

00:50:25.770 --> 00:50:30.670
I think with that, we will go
to questions from the audience.

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:35.020
There are some folks
with microphones.

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:39.600
And so if you could raise your
hands and they will come to you

00:50:39.600 --> 00:50:43.380
and let you use those to
address a question to the panel.

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:44.780
I see several hands over there.

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:47.730
I'll let you guys
just figure that out.

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:48.990
Please, sir.

00:50:48.990 --> 00:50:53.460
AUDIENCE: My question involves
whether the other two gentlemen

00:50:53.460 --> 00:50:58.290
agree or disagree
with Mr. Sullivan

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:02.340
that capitalism has been
an unabashed failure

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:04.350
in the Western nations.

00:51:04.350 --> 00:51:07.710
I don't feel that--

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:09.810
I feel that the
younger generation

00:51:09.810 --> 00:51:12.360
has had great
difficulties because

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:14.880
of the increase
in college tuition

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:17.320
and some of those aspects.

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:20.700
But to call it an unabashed
failure in the West

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:22.737
astounds me.

00:51:22.737 --> 00:51:24.570
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Can I
just respond to that?

00:51:27.090 --> 00:51:29.530
Obviously, capitalism has
been an incredible success.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:30.990
If you look at the entire world.

00:51:30.990 --> 00:51:33.240
And it's certainly been a
success in the last 20 years

00:51:33.240 --> 00:51:37.740
for large numbers of people
in the developing world.

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:41.430
It is not working
for middle class

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:44.580
and working class Westerners.

00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:48.360
It is actually making
it incredibly hard

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:51.330
for them to scrape
together a living.

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:56.070
The data is simply unanswerable.

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:59.160
The idea that a capitalist
economy will benefit everyone

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:03.910
has disappeared because
it is not true anymore.

00:52:03.910 --> 00:52:07.650
And I think people on the right
need to get real about that.

00:52:07.650 --> 00:52:10.190
That's why we have Trump.

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:13.630
That's why we have
a rejection of what

00:52:13.630 --> 00:52:15.880
you might call neoliberalism
because in the end,

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:18.650
it was a victim of
its own success.

00:52:18.650 --> 00:52:22.960
And the forces of mass
migration and of globalization

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:26.500
and of free trade
have essentially

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:31.060
decimated the potential for
ordinary people in the West

00:52:31.060 --> 00:52:33.700
to actually earn a
living and to see

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:36.880
the living standards of their
kids be higher than their own.

00:52:36.880 --> 00:52:38.560
And right now, their
living standards

00:52:38.560 --> 00:52:39.910
are going to be lower.

00:52:39.910 --> 00:52:42.320
And that is not a success.

00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:42.890
I'm sorry.

00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:44.530
It's not a success in the last--

00:52:44.530 --> 00:52:47.020
we're talking about this
particular period in time,

00:52:47.020 --> 00:52:48.310
and it's really hurting.

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:52.910
That's why our entire
politics are the way they are.

00:52:52.910 --> 00:52:55.640
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: To me, the
answer that Andrew just

00:52:55.640 --> 00:52:59.180
gave now as opposed
to his earlier answer

00:52:59.180 --> 00:53:00.860
is very close to my own view.

00:53:00.860 --> 00:53:05.150
Look, if you look at the
numbers that Donald Trump cites

00:53:05.150 --> 00:53:09.650
in each of his speeches,
it's hard to disagree

00:53:09.650 --> 00:53:13.640
with his reports about
lowest unemployment

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:18.390
rate, highest levels of
employment for minorities.

00:53:18.390 --> 00:53:20.900
And it's hard to
look at those numbers

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:23.660
and say that we have been
anything but successful.

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:26.510
When you look at the
poll data, his rating

00:53:26.510 --> 00:53:29.750
on handling the economy
is about 10 points,

00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:32.860
12 points higher than his
overall approval rating.

00:53:32.860 --> 00:53:33.950
But Andrew's right.

00:53:33.950 --> 00:53:38.840
It would be absolutely wrong
to conclude from those numbers

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:45.080
that we've had the level of
upward mobility and reduction

00:53:45.080 --> 00:53:49.430
in income inequality that
a healthy society sees.

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:54.980
To me, the reason I talked about
jobs in my initial presentation

00:53:54.980 --> 00:53:57.710
is the Democrats
need a narrative that

00:53:57.710 --> 00:54:01.490
is both pro-capitalism,
pro-growth.

00:54:01.490 --> 00:54:04.580
I think it's what Bill,
if I can flatter him again

00:54:04.580 --> 00:54:07.820
with plagiarism, called
inclusive growth.

00:54:07.820 --> 00:54:10.430
And I think if the
Democrats are able to do

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:14.090
that as part of an overarching
strategy that doesn't

00:54:14.090 --> 00:54:17.850
necessarily focus
exclusively on culture,

00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:26.550
they will be inestimably
stronger in November 2020.

00:54:26.550 --> 00:54:28.380
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
My view is that if you

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:30.690
look at the history
of market economies

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:34.410
extending back
hundreds of years,

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:40.650
they go through periodic
episodes of crisis

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:42.630
or deep challenge.

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:45.550
And the task in each case--

00:54:45.550 --> 00:54:48.660
and it has been
successful up to now--

00:54:48.660 --> 00:54:55.510
is to make the necessary
policy adjustments

00:54:55.510 --> 00:54:58.580
in the framework
of market economies

00:54:58.580 --> 00:55:03.940
so that they do achieve
their objectives, including

00:55:03.940 --> 00:55:06.920
widespread benefits
to the populations,

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:11.830
not just of developing countries
but of developed countries

00:55:11.830 --> 00:55:16.570
I believe that in the wake of
the economic crisis of 2008,

00:55:16.570 --> 00:55:22.840
2009, we are again in
one of those periods.

00:55:22.840 --> 00:55:25.870
The argument within
the Democratic Party

00:55:25.870 --> 00:55:29.860
is whether capitalism should
be adjusted or abandoned.

00:55:29.860 --> 00:55:32.770
I confidently predict
at the end of the day

00:55:32.770 --> 00:55:37.090
that the market economy in the
United States will be adjusted,

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:39.230
not abandoned.

00:55:39.230 --> 00:55:41.290
There will be
responses to a series

00:55:41.290 --> 00:55:43.600
of particular
challenges, including

00:55:43.600 --> 00:55:47.860
the cost of health
care, the cost of higher

00:55:47.860 --> 00:55:53.320
education, the fact that
many young people today,

00:55:53.320 --> 00:55:56.830
even in their 30s, view the
prospect of home ownership

00:55:56.830 --> 00:55:59.230
as almost unimaginably
distant, which

00:55:59.230 --> 00:56:03.820
is a profound transformation
from the way things were.

00:56:03.820 --> 00:56:10.380
In other words, we're
not in the 1990s anymore,

00:56:10.380 --> 00:56:16.980
and the optimism that we had
about having a winning hand

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:19.470
and needing only to
play it out reasonably

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:23.730
skillfully has dissipated,
I think, for good reasons.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:28.080
And we now have to think more
deeply about the adjustments

00:56:28.080 --> 00:56:31.770
to the market economy
that are necessary.

00:56:31.770 --> 00:56:34.860
Many Republican
thinkers, for example,

00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:39.900
are talking about systems of
tax credits or wage supplements

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:42.870
for low and moderate
income workers.

00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:45.030
Not to put too
fine a point on it,

00:56:45.030 --> 00:56:48.870
that is a form of redistribution
within the market system.

00:56:48.870 --> 00:56:53.580
Many Democrats agree
with that approach.

00:56:53.580 --> 00:56:58.560
I would also say, in conclusion,
that the government every year

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:03.930
publishes a comprehensive
report on income and poverty.

00:57:03.930 --> 00:57:06.630
And the report that
came out just last week

00:57:06.630 --> 00:57:12.210
showed that, corrected for
inflation, the median earnings

00:57:12.210 --> 00:57:16.320
of men working full
time, year round have not

00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:20.710
increased since 1973.

00:57:20.710 --> 00:57:23.010
Those are official government--

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:24.760
ANDREW SULLIVAN: That
is a failure, right?

00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:26.980
That is a failure.

00:57:26.980 --> 00:57:31.000
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: That
is, I would say, evidence,

00:57:31.000 --> 00:57:34.810
at the very least, that
something has gone wrong

00:57:34.810 --> 00:57:36.700
that needs to be fixed.

00:57:36.700 --> 00:57:41.870
Whether socialism is the name
of the fix I am very skeptical.

00:57:41.870 --> 00:57:43.120
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Me too, Bill.

00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:44.038
I'm not advocating.

00:57:44.038 --> 00:57:45.830
I'm just trying to
analyze what's going on.

00:57:45.830 --> 00:57:46.955
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Me too.

00:57:49.522 --> 00:57:51.730
ANDREW SULLIVAN: I would
support Joe Biden over that.

00:57:51.730 --> 00:57:54.880
But I'm just trying
to explain why

00:57:54.880 --> 00:57:59.350
I think the forces that have
swept over people, which

00:57:59.350 --> 00:58:02.980
they're responding to by
very understandably wanting

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:06.160
to curtail free trade, wanting
to curtail immigration,

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:13.090
wanting to make themselves feel
more secure in this economy,

00:58:13.090 --> 00:58:15.500
is one of the central
issues of our time.

00:58:15.500 --> 00:58:17.500
It's defining all
of our politics.

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:20.720
And again, I think if it
weren't for immigration

00:58:20.720 --> 00:58:23.740
and if it weren't for
their cultural Marxism,

00:58:23.740 --> 00:58:26.770
Dems could do really well
and adjust capitalism, just

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:30.220
in a more redistributive way.

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:32.980
WILLIAM A. GALSTON:
Well, I think

00:58:32.980 --> 00:58:34.570
we largely agree on that point.

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:36.870
Whether we agree on
the underlying analysis

00:58:36.870 --> 00:58:38.090
is interesting.

00:58:38.090 --> 00:58:45.620
So for example, let's take
full time male workers

00:58:45.620 --> 00:58:48.200
as an analytical baseline.

00:58:48.200 --> 00:58:51.920
Two big things that have
happened in the past half

00:58:51.920 --> 00:58:53.180
century.

00:58:53.180 --> 00:58:56.900
First of all, the surge of
women into the paid workforce--

00:58:56.900 --> 00:58:59.060
that is domestic competition.

00:58:59.060 --> 00:59:00.830
And secondly, the
creation of what

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:03.180
amounts to a global
labor market,

00:59:03.180 --> 00:59:07.140
which did not exist until
the collapse of communism,

00:59:07.140 --> 00:59:10.610
the fall of the Berlin Wall,
and the entrance of China

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:13.830
fully into the global economy.

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:22.370
I think we have not yet begun
to think through how countries

00:59:22.370 --> 00:59:24.710
like the United
States can respond

00:59:24.710 --> 00:59:30.290
to huge tectonic economic
forces like that.

00:59:30.290 --> 00:59:33.080
And I don't think
those responses are

00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:37.490
going to look a lot like the
1990s, my favorite decade,

00:59:37.490 --> 00:59:40.550
or the 1980s, presumably
the favorite decade of a lot

00:59:40.550 --> 00:59:42.700
of the people in this room.

00:59:42.700 --> 00:59:43.700
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: OK.

00:59:43.700 --> 00:59:46.360
Let's get another question
over here, please.

00:59:49.157 --> 00:59:51.490
AUDIENCE: Thank you, gentlemen,
for participating today.

00:59:51.490 --> 00:59:53.950
And this kind of follows
the first question

00:59:53.950 --> 00:59:58.690
is, if the left is in
an inexorable movement

00:59:58.690 --> 01:00:04.120
to socialism, the
phrase of something

01:00:04.120 --> 01:00:07.360
about the definition of
lunacy is doing the same thing

01:00:07.360 --> 01:00:09.010
and expecting a
different result,

01:00:09.010 --> 01:00:12.760
how do socialists on the left
expect a different result

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:15.700
from every instance we've
seen of socialism, i.e.

01:00:15.700 --> 01:00:20.560
the Soviet Union and its
demise and Venezuela's sinking

01:00:20.560 --> 01:00:22.870
into a pit of--

01:00:22.870 --> 01:00:26.200
if I were to quote my
president, a shithole.

01:00:28.920 --> 01:00:31.920
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Well,
to put it as simply as--

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:34.830
I can make my answer very short.

01:00:34.830 --> 01:00:37.320
I do not see the
Democratic Party

01:00:37.320 --> 01:00:41.890
as being on an inexorable
path towards socialism.

01:00:41.890 --> 01:00:47.010
I see it as being in the
middle of a debate as to how

01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:52.900
contemporary capitalism
can be adjusted

01:00:52.900 --> 01:00:57.220
so that the general
welfare is better promoted

01:00:57.220 --> 01:00:58.760
than under the status quo.

01:00:58.760 --> 01:01:00.400
That's the real debate.

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:03.190
That's the
Constitutional debate.

01:01:03.190 --> 01:01:05.620
What does the general
welfare require

01:01:05.620 --> 01:01:07.540
in current circumstances?

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:15.490
The Democratic Party will
never arrive at Jeremy Corbyn's

01:01:15.490 --> 01:01:18.750
solution, never.

01:01:18.750 --> 01:01:20.500
ANDREW SULLIVAN: That's
what the Blairites

01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:22.690
said before it happened.

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:25.700
I'm just trying to
explain it, understand it.

01:01:25.700 --> 01:01:26.200
That's all.

01:01:26.200 --> 01:01:28.240
I just think it's a
very fertile time.

01:01:28.240 --> 01:01:31.750
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: I think if you
look at the rhetoric of Bernie

01:01:31.750 --> 01:01:38.740
Sanders and Elizabeth Warren,
who were getting 40% together

01:01:38.740 --> 01:01:42.580
in the Wall Street Journal
poll out today, higher

01:01:42.580 --> 01:01:45.880
with their combined
vote than Biden's 30%,

01:01:45.880 --> 01:01:50.410
their rhetoric is explicitly
socialist, their solutions

01:01:50.410 --> 01:01:52.940
clearly socialist.

01:01:52.940 --> 01:01:54.990
And I think Bill's right.

01:01:54.990 --> 01:01:58.880
I don't think capitalism
is going away anytime soon,

01:01:58.880 --> 01:02:02.770
but we're going to have a
robust debate of the type

01:02:02.770 --> 01:02:05.700
that I think, even a
few years ago, would

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:07.360
have been unimaginable.

01:02:07.360 --> 01:02:13.800
And I think a good part of the
reason is what Andrew suggests.

01:02:13.800 --> 01:02:18.460
But let me just offer a
slightly more straightforward

01:02:18.460 --> 01:02:20.320
explanation.

01:02:20.320 --> 01:02:24.760
When we all got out of
school, the presumption--

01:02:24.760 --> 01:02:30.010
at least in my world and what
I saw when I came of age--

01:02:30.010 --> 01:02:32.800
was that there were
limitless possibilities.

01:02:32.800 --> 01:02:34.590
Tuition was cheap.

01:02:34.590 --> 01:02:38.290
Health care was
available and affordable

01:02:38.290 --> 01:02:40.090
through private means.

01:02:40.090 --> 01:02:44.110
Or even if you paid yourself,
it didn't cost you much.

01:02:44.110 --> 01:02:49.150
Jobs were plentifully available,
opportunities for advancement

01:02:49.150 --> 01:02:50.530
clear.

01:02:50.530 --> 01:02:52.330
And that's all disappeared.

01:02:52.330 --> 01:02:57.640
And I see lots of young people
running a business as, I think,

01:02:57.640 --> 01:03:01.090
the only full time
capitalist on this panel,

01:03:01.090 --> 01:03:06.760
and the degree of
uncertainty that I see

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:08.710
and the degree of
desperation that I

01:03:08.710 --> 01:03:11.050
see of people with
loans they can't

01:03:11.050 --> 01:03:18.730
pay for jobs they are unable to
procure in a society that does

01:03:18.730 --> 01:03:25.240
not offer upward mobility, as
Bill suggested, I think gives

01:03:25.240 --> 01:03:30.550
rise to a level of frustration
that goes well beyond going

01:03:30.550 --> 01:03:34.750
to Constitutional
principles, and raises

01:03:34.750 --> 01:03:38.240
real issues of
anger, frustration,

01:03:38.240 --> 01:03:43.090
and bitterness in a country
with changing demographics,

01:03:43.090 --> 01:03:46.870
with a population that
is restive in ways

01:03:46.870 --> 01:03:50.410
that we had never
anticipated see

01:03:50.410 --> 01:03:54.610
mass incarceration, a new
civil rights movement.

01:03:54.610 --> 01:03:56.740
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Also
notice that the person who

01:03:56.740 --> 01:04:00.130
ran as the candidate the
present for the Republican Party

01:04:00.130 --> 01:04:05.620
last time was in favor of all
the entitlements actually going

01:04:05.620 --> 01:04:07.990
up, told people how they
could have free health

01:04:07.990 --> 01:04:10.030
care that would be cheap.

01:04:10.030 --> 01:04:11.710
He just had some answer to it.

01:04:11.710 --> 01:04:15.400
He ran-- I mean, he was
lying the entire time,

01:04:15.400 --> 01:04:19.470
but he ran on a far more
progressive platform,

01:04:19.470 --> 01:04:23.920
and also against free trade,
than any previous Republican

01:04:23.920 --> 01:04:25.450
ever had.

01:04:25.450 --> 01:04:29.500
So if the Republican Party has
turned that left on economics,

01:04:29.500 --> 01:04:33.040
what do you expect the
Democratic Party to do?

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:36.550
Now, it's obscured because
the Republican Party right now

01:04:36.550 --> 01:04:43.540
is just this cult. And because
in power, the Republican Party

01:04:43.540 --> 01:04:44.920
did the reverse.

01:04:44.920 --> 01:04:47.410
It actually intensified
social inequality

01:04:47.410 --> 01:04:50.590
and intensified climate
change deliberately, kind

01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:52.240
of mind blowing.

01:04:52.240 --> 01:04:55.690
But in the politics
of it, they definitely

01:04:55.690 --> 01:04:58.130
tacked left this time.

01:04:58.130 --> 01:04:59.130
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: OK.

01:04:59.130 --> 01:05:00.047
Next question, please.

01:05:02.930 --> 01:05:04.400
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:05:04.400 --> 01:05:10.170
I grew up in high school and
college in the early '70s.

01:05:10.170 --> 01:05:15.750
And when I was in school, there
was a huge leftist movement

01:05:15.750 --> 01:05:20.610
going on at the time,
anti-war, anti-Vietnam.

01:05:20.610 --> 01:05:23.280
Youth, the Baby Boomers,
were the largest generation

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:28.050
to come forward, and everyone
predicted a seismic shift

01:05:28.050 --> 01:05:31.230
in politics to the left.

01:05:31.230 --> 01:05:36.120
I recall the election of 1972
with a fairly unpopular Richard

01:05:36.120 --> 01:05:42.760
Nixon in the press and among
the colleges and the elite,

01:05:42.760 --> 01:05:45.430
and I remember a
professor of history

01:05:45.430 --> 01:05:50.700
from Wisconsin, George McGovern,
being the vaunted savior,

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:54.300
and income equality
and income guarantees

01:05:54.300 --> 01:05:56.310
being a substantial
part of his platform

01:05:56.310 --> 01:06:01.200
as well as being what, in
fact, he was promoting.

01:06:01.200 --> 01:06:04.140
Now I see Elizabeth Warren,
I see Bernie Sanders,

01:06:04.140 --> 01:06:06.400
and I see Donald Trump.

01:06:06.400 --> 01:06:11.290
And your projection, Andrew,
about the inevitability

01:06:11.290 --> 01:06:14.080
of the Millennials
turning left reminds

01:06:14.080 --> 01:06:19.130
me of another Tory, Winston
Churchill, and his comment

01:06:19.130 --> 01:06:26.300
about those in their youth not
being left versus those as they

01:06:26.300 --> 01:06:28.670
come of age not being right.

01:06:28.670 --> 01:06:33.110
I don't see the inevitability
of the Millennial generation

01:06:33.110 --> 01:06:37.400
and the success of Z
generation remaining left.

01:06:37.400 --> 01:06:42.080
I think the reality is that
conservative nationalism is

01:06:42.080 --> 01:06:46.170
a progression that is bringing
great results in this country.

01:06:46.170 --> 01:06:49.743
How do you address the
historical paradigm?

01:06:49.743 --> 01:06:51.160
DOUGLAS SCHOEN:
Well, Nixon didn't

01:06:51.160 --> 01:06:52.900
govern as a right winger.

01:06:52.900 --> 01:06:54.190
That's for sure.

01:06:54.190 --> 01:06:57.940
If you look at his record,
many of us on the left,

01:06:57.940 --> 01:07:01.090
when you strip out
Watergate and you strip out

01:07:01.090 --> 01:07:07.750
the unpleasant nature of
much of what Nixon said,

01:07:07.750 --> 01:07:14.170
would be very happy with a
economic and social record that

01:07:14.170 --> 01:07:17.830
was quite moderate, hence
his 49-state victory.

01:07:17.830 --> 01:07:22.420
And I would just ask you to
turn your clock a little forward

01:07:22.420 --> 01:07:26.380
to the '74 election when
there was a sea change,

01:07:26.380 --> 01:07:30.670
and even to '76 when
Jimmy Carter was

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:33.820
elected as a southern
governor promising

01:07:33.820 --> 01:07:35.110
he would never lie to us.

01:07:35.110 --> 01:07:39.220
Now, I'm not suggesting
that Jimmy Carter represents

01:07:39.220 --> 01:07:41.950
what we in the Democratic
Party stand for.

01:07:41.950 --> 01:07:44.110
He most assuredly does not.

01:07:44.110 --> 01:07:46.570
But there was a
dramatic reaction

01:07:46.570 --> 01:07:55.510
in the '70s against what the
Nixon presidency produced.

01:07:55.510 --> 01:07:59.170
And indeed, because Nixon
governed as a centrist,

01:07:59.170 --> 01:08:04.060
we ended up in 1980 with a
continuation of a movement

01:08:04.060 --> 01:08:09.490
in the Republican Party
started by Barry Goldwater

01:08:09.490 --> 01:08:10.130
to the right.

01:08:10.130 --> 01:08:13.450
You had Proposition
13 in 1978, and you

01:08:13.450 --> 01:08:17.680
had the nomination and election
of Ronald Reagan in 1980.

01:08:17.680 --> 01:08:23.359
So as I see that history, I see
it quite different than you do.

01:08:23.359 --> 01:08:25.510
But I would also
tell you that it's

01:08:25.510 --> 01:08:29.260
going to be pretty hard for
me, as I look at the poll data

01:08:29.260 --> 01:08:32.620
and see the Millennials
2 to 1 left, also

01:08:32.620 --> 01:08:37.270
with no strength of their
Democratic allegiance--

01:08:37.270 --> 01:08:39.700
a lot of them call
themselves independents not

01:08:39.700 --> 01:08:42.310
because they're
really independent,

01:08:42.310 --> 01:08:45.370
but because they're not
aligned with any of the two

01:08:45.370 --> 01:08:46.640
major parties.

01:08:46.640 --> 01:08:49.000
So I think if you
just try to believe

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:53.109
that political nationalism
of the type Trump is pushing

01:08:53.109 --> 01:08:56.740
is going to command a
majority of the Millennials,

01:08:56.740 --> 01:08:59.120
I think you're
overstating the case.

01:08:59.120 --> 01:09:01.120
ANDREW SULLIVAN: And let
me add one other thing,

01:09:01.120 --> 01:09:04.720
that for the first time,
the Republican Party now

01:09:04.720 --> 01:09:09.133
stands for massive borrowing.

01:09:09.133 --> 01:09:11.050
The one thing that
socialism was always about,

01:09:11.050 --> 01:09:13.210
as Margaret Thatcher said, was
spending other people's money.

01:09:13.210 --> 01:09:14.920
But there has never
been a president

01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:18.729
who has spent other people's
money quite as wantonly

01:09:18.729 --> 01:09:20.270
as the current one.

01:09:20.270 --> 01:09:23.080
Now, it's perfectly
massive amounts

01:09:23.080 --> 01:09:27.729
of spending and huge tax
cuts to the super wealthy.

01:09:27.729 --> 01:09:30.490
That's what he's done.

01:09:30.490 --> 01:09:32.140
When you have said,
it doesn't matter

01:09:32.140 --> 01:09:33.729
what the budget deficit is.

01:09:33.729 --> 01:09:37.029
We can run a trillion
dollar deficit every year,

01:09:37.029 --> 01:09:41.170
and we know the Republican
Party will still cheer us.

01:09:41.170 --> 01:09:43.609
Of course, there's an opening
on the left to say, well,

01:09:43.609 --> 01:09:46.149
if there's all that money
around you just found,

01:09:46.149 --> 01:09:49.029
even though we were massively
in debt when you came to office

01:09:49.029 --> 01:09:51.250
and we were in a
period of growth,

01:09:51.250 --> 01:09:54.970
and you still borrowed all that
money to throw at the economy,

01:09:54.970 --> 01:09:57.610
why can't you-- and just
for the very wealthy--

01:09:57.610 --> 01:09:59.560
why can't you do
it for the poor?

01:09:59.560 --> 01:10:01.440
Why can't you do it
for the working class?

01:10:01.440 --> 01:10:03.680
That's such a powerful argument.

01:10:03.680 --> 01:10:07.000
And I think that the Republicans
have completely ceded

01:10:07.000 --> 01:10:08.740
the party of
responsibility, as it

01:10:08.740 --> 01:10:11.080
were, the party of,
we can't afford that,

01:10:11.080 --> 01:10:13.450
the party of, no, we
have to have limits.

01:10:13.450 --> 01:10:15.620
That is not the current
Republican Party,

01:10:15.620 --> 01:10:17.620
actually hasn't been
for a very long time.

01:10:17.620 --> 01:10:19.570
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Let
me, since Andrew and I

01:10:19.570 --> 01:10:21.280
have disagreed on
some points, let

01:10:21.280 --> 01:10:25.750
me agree emphatically with him
on the point that he just made.

01:10:25.750 --> 01:10:30.040
And here is the
bill of particulars.

01:10:30.040 --> 01:10:35.110
I think we all remember then
candidate Donald Trump's

01:10:35.110 --> 01:10:39.970
famous down the escalator
announcement speech.

01:10:39.970 --> 01:10:43.180
At least, we remember what
he said about Mexicans.

01:10:43.180 --> 01:10:44.920
At least, I do.

01:10:44.920 --> 01:10:48.130
What we forget is something
that he said later

01:10:48.130 --> 01:10:49.630
in the announcement
speech, and you

01:10:49.630 --> 01:10:52.360
can check it for
yourselves, namely,

01:10:52.360 --> 01:10:57.310
that we have to save Social
Security and Medicare

01:10:57.310 --> 01:11:00.190
and Medicaid "without cuts."

01:11:00.190 --> 01:11:01.340
I quote.

01:11:01.340 --> 01:11:05.020
And then he went on to
say, we've got to do it.

01:11:05.020 --> 01:11:12.820
And an important part of the
meaning of Donald Trump's

01:11:12.820 --> 01:11:18.370
nomination and election
was the direct repudiation

01:11:18.370 --> 01:11:22.210
of the economic
and fiscal policies

01:11:22.210 --> 01:11:24.850
of the previous leader
of the Republican Party,

01:11:24.850 --> 01:11:27.790
namely Paul Ryan.

01:11:27.790 --> 01:11:31.000
Trumpism on economics
is the rejection

01:11:31.000 --> 01:11:36.610
of Ryanism on entitlements,
on the budget deficit,

01:11:36.610 --> 01:11:37.215
and on trade.

01:11:40.670 --> 01:11:44.800
And that move in
the Republican Party

01:11:44.800 --> 01:11:50.080
has fundamentally reshaped the
terrain of American politics

01:11:50.080 --> 01:11:53.080
and the competition
between the two parties.

01:11:53.080 --> 01:11:54.790
And I think we're
just beginning to work

01:11:54.790 --> 01:11:57.400
through the
implications of that.

01:11:57.400 --> 01:12:02.170
Whether we can live with
economic policies that

01:12:02.170 --> 01:12:05.800
are indifferent to
the sorts of things

01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:08.500
that Paul Ryan was trying
to do something about

01:12:08.500 --> 01:12:13.752
is another question altogether,
but that's where we are.

01:12:13.752 --> 01:12:16.085
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: We have
time for one more question.

01:12:20.140 --> 01:12:20.860
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:12:20.860 --> 01:12:24.110
Thank you for this very
interesting debate.

01:12:24.110 --> 01:12:26.460
I did not go to
Hillsdale College.

01:12:26.460 --> 01:12:28.150
We support Hillsdale.

01:12:28.150 --> 01:12:30.250
I am probably the last
person that should

01:12:30.250 --> 01:12:32.490
get up and talk about politics.

01:12:32.490 --> 01:12:34.000
So I will not.

01:12:34.000 --> 01:12:36.640
The title of this
conference today

01:12:36.640 --> 01:12:39.700
was The Future of
the Democratic Party.

01:12:39.700 --> 01:12:41.740
I have heard the word Marxism.

01:12:41.740 --> 01:12:44.360
I hear the word socialism.

01:12:44.360 --> 01:12:46.720
I agree with you, sir.

01:12:46.720 --> 01:12:51.220
There has been no one of the
27,000 Democratic people that

01:12:51.220 --> 01:12:54.670
are up there talking
about a platform

01:12:54.670 --> 01:12:58.990
that I learned back in somewhat,
I think, my younger years.

01:12:58.990 --> 01:13:02.950
So I ask you, if you are
going to end this, tell me,

01:13:02.950 --> 01:13:07.810
is socialism for the
Democratic Party the future?

01:13:07.810 --> 01:13:09.970
And is that socialism defined?

01:13:09.970 --> 01:13:14.440
Because that also can be, when
it's on steroids, communism,

01:13:14.440 --> 01:13:19.300
and that is something we fought
for years to rid ourselves of.

01:13:19.300 --> 01:13:22.540
So I ask you with your
intelligence, your study,

01:13:22.540 --> 01:13:25.240
and your backgrounds,
what is the future

01:13:25.240 --> 01:13:27.880
of the Democratic Party?

01:13:27.880 --> 01:13:29.760
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:34.250
DOUGLAS SCHOEN: I'll take
a shot at that quickly.

01:13:34.250 --> 01:13:40.210
I think we are going to see the
advocacy of socialist policies

01:13:40.210 --> 01:13:45.130
by the Democrats, by their
left wing, by the Squad,

01:13:45.130 --> 01:13:47.980
by Bernie Sanders,
Elizabeth Warren,

01:13:47.980 --> 01:13:51.670
continue unabated through
this election whether we win,

01:13:51.670 --> 01:13:53.560
lose, or draw.

01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:56.770
I don't think that we're going
to be a communist society.

01:13:56.770 --> 01:14:00.280
I think there's very little
chance of that, though I didn't

01:14:00.280 --> 01:14:03.580
think Venezuela was going
to end up with Maduro,

01:14:03.580 --> 01:14:06.640
either, though I see no
evidence that his cronies will

01:14:06.640 --> 01:14:10.300
do anything but end up in
our federal penitentiaries

01:14:10.300 --> 01:14:13.240
where many of them belong
for drug trafficking.

01:14:13.240 --> 01:14:16.960
That being said, capitalism
isn't going away here.

01:14:16.960 --> 01:14:19.360
I think Bill's largely
right, that it will

01:14:19.360 --> 01:14:22.060
face challenges and survive.

01:14:22.060 --> 01:14:24.310
My point is only
those challenges

01:14:24.310 --> 01:14:27.430
are going to be more pronounced,
more continual, and more

01:14:27.430 --> 01:14:31.180
vexatious than I think most
Democrats, and certainly

01:14:31.180 --> 01:14:34.577
this Democrat,
would like to see.

01:14:34.577 --> 01:14:36.160
ANDREW SULLIVAN: I
don't think I would

01:14:36.160 --> 01:14:42.280
call the proposals of, say,
Elizabeth Warren socialism,

01:14:42.280 --> 01:14:42.985
and this is why.

01:14:45.900 --> 01:14:49.590
Do you think Margaret
Thatcher was a socialist?

01:14:49.590 --> 01:14:53.430
She presided over free health
care for absolutely everyone

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:56.220
and defended it through
a socialist delivery

01:14:56.220 --> 01:14:58.215
system for three terms.

01:15:00.780 --> 01:15:03.930
Was Britain socialist
under Margaret Thatcher?

01:15:03.930 --> 01:15:05.100
I don't think so.

01:15:05.100 --> 01:15:10.200
Is public availability
of health care popular?

01:15:10.200 --> 01:15:13.470
Hugely popular.

01:15:13.470 --> 01:15:14.880
What is Boris Johnson doing now?

01:15:14.880 --> 01:15:17.100
Promising to spend
millions and millions

01:15:17.100 --> 01:15:19.410
and millions of dollars
on the health care system

01:15:19.410 --> 01:15:22.080
so that everyone can have
access to health care.

01:15:22.080 --> 01:15:26.760
Universal access to health
care is not socialism.

01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:31.180
It has been the foundation of
every other Western society.

01:15:31.180 --> 01:15:37.940
But I do think that it will
become more redistributionist,

01:15:37.940 --> 01:15:40.780
and I do think it's going
to become more leftist,

01:15:40.780 --> 01:15:44.080
and I do think it's
going to culturally,

01:15:44.080 --> 01:15:46.540
because the right has done
such a terrible job of engaging

01:15:46.540 --> 01:15:48.865
the next generations,
is going to be

01:15:48.865 --> 01:15:50.740
much more culturally
and socially to the left

01:15:50.740 --> 01:15:54.380
than we've ever seen before.

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:57.080
WILLIAM A. GALSTON: Well,
I guess it leaves it

01:15:57.080 --> 01:16:00.470
to me to end this discussion.

01:16:00.470 --> 01:16:04.790
I think I've been pretty
clear that I don't think

01:16:04.790 --> 01:16:08.510
that the future of the
country is socialist,

01:16:08.510 --> 01:16:09.690
let alone communist.

01:16:09.690 --> 01:16:12.020
And I don't think the future
of the Democratic Party

01:16:12.020 --> 01:16:15.620
is that, either.

01:16:15.620 --> 01:16:18.350
What we are going through
right now within the party

01:16:18.350 --> 01:16:21.900
and within the
country is an effort--

01:16:21.900 --> 01:16:25.040
and I think this is what our
party system is all about--

01:16:25.040 --> 01:16:29.015
to struggle with very
real public problems.

01:16:32.930 --> 01:16:38.900
If the economy is not
working well for everybody--

01:16:38.900 --> 01:16:41.480
by the way, if it had been
working well for everybody,

01:16:41.480 --> 01:16:43.400
Donald Trump would
never have been elected

01:16:43.400 --> 01:16:45.290
president of the United States.

01:16:45.290 --> 01:16:51.260
He represented a
response to the failure

01:16:51.260 --> 01:16:53.660
of the country over
a period of decades

01:16:53.660 --> 01:16:56.510
under Republican and
Democratic presidents

01:16:56.510 --> 01:16:59.930
to deal with the problems
afflicting the manufacturing

01:16:59.930 --> 01:17:02.690
sector of the country,
to deal with the problems

01:17:02.690 --> 01:17:05.900
of rural America, the
absence of capital investment

01:17:05.900 --> 01:17:07.490
in those areas, et cetera.

01:17:07.490 --> 01:17:16.580
He represented one response to
a very uneven economic terrain.

01:17:16.580 --> 01:17:18.980
And the Democrats
are now wrestling

01:17:18.980 --> 01:17:25.070
about what their response to the
same set of problems should be.

01:17:25.070 --> 01:17:29.420
We will not end up with
socialism on the left.

01:17:29.420 --> 01:17:33.500
We will not end up with
fascism on the right.

01:17:33.500 --> 01:17:36.020
But within 10 years,
I think we will

01:17:36.020 --> 01:17:41.900
see very significant adjustments
to the market system, much more

01:17:41.900 --> 01:17:44.390
aggressive antitrust policy.

01:17:44.390 --> 01:17:48.650
Yes, I agree with Andrew, more
emphasis on redistribution

01:17:48.650 --> 01:17:50.930
for those people who
are working full time

01:17:50.930 --> 01:17:54.410
and not making it
in today's economy.

01:17:54.410 --> 01:18:00.380
We are likely to see a
real adjustment of finance

01:18:00.380 --> 01:18:03.860
for higher education,
which is now in trouble

01:18:03.860 --> 01:18:06.890
because, in response to
the financial crisis,

01:18:06.890 --> 01:18:10.850
virtually every state responded
with huge cuts in subsidies

01:18:10.850 --> 01:18:13.070
to higher education,
shifting those burdens

01:18:13.070 --> 01:18:14.930
to students and their families.

01:18:14.930 --> 01:18:18.800
Those are the real debates
we're going to have.

01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:25.110
And I think that hundreds of
years of bettors have gone

01:18:25.110 --> 01:18:29.700
broke betting against the
capacity of the United States

01:18:29.700 --> 01:18:33.840
to assess and, as
Winston Churchill put it,

01:18:33.840 --> 01:18:36.270
when all other
alternatives are exhausted,

01:18:36.270 --> 01:18:37.600
to do the right thing.

01:18:37.600 --> 01:18:38.638
Thank you very much.

01:18:38.638 --> 01:18:39.930
RONALD J. PESTRITTO: Thank you.

01:18:44.950 --> 01:18:49.030
So we're going to
reconvene at 10:30.

01:18:49.030 --> 01:18:52.240
So please enjoy a
break in the interim,

01:18:52.240 --> 01:18:54.550
and join me in thanking
these gentlemen

01:18:54.550 --> 01:18:57.700
for giving us some very
good things to think about.

01:18:57.700 --> 01:19:00.750
[APPLAUSE]

